WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.845
Follow a request from the network through a queue to
application logic and back as a response.

00:00:05.845 --> 00:00:09.948
The queue-and-worker model makes concurrency and
overload visible.

00:00:09.948 --> 00:00:14.860
HTTP supplies a common contract, while frameworks
handle much of the mechanics.

00:00:14.860 --> 00:00:20.580
Remember that asynchronous servers can implement the
same service without a thread for every

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:21.078
request.

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:25.415
Trace a wiki request through browser, application
and database.

00:00:25.415 --> 00:00:32.127
MediaWiki reads article data, renders it and adds
presentation and possibly user-specific content.

00:00:32.127 --> 00:00:36.921
This example connects web protocols to architectural
responsibilities.

00:00:36.921 --> 00:00:43.701
It also sets up two important questions: which state
must be shared, and which repeated work can be

00:00:43.701 --> 00:00:44.660
safely cached?
